Shoot shell
==========

Start an interactive shell with:

    shoot

Run a script and exit with its result:

    shoot FILE.shoot

Use shoot --help for the short invocation summary. help inside Shoot lists
Shoot's built-in commands. Use a native command's -h or --help for its
quick usage. This manual describes Shoot as an application; built-ins do not
have separate manual pages.

Interactive command editing
---------------------------

The prompt displays the current identity and working directory. A home path is
shown as ~. Input uses a nonmodal line editor:

  Left / Right             Move by one UTF-8 scalar.
  Up / Down                Browse history; returning to the newest position
                           restores the unfinished command draft.
  Alt+Left / Alt+Right     Move to the previous / next word.
  Home / End               Move to the start / end of the command.
  Backspace / Delete       Delete the previous / next scalar.
  Alt+Backspace / Alt+Delete
                           Delete to the previous / next word boundary.
  Tab                      Complete a command or built-in at command position;
                           otherwise complete a path.
  Enter                    Execute the command.
  Ctrl+C                   Discard the input and return to a fresh prompt.
  Ctrl+D                   On an empty line, return end-of-file. Otherwise,
                           delete the scalar under the cursor if present.
  Ctrl+L                   Clear the terminal and redraw the prompt and input.

Shoot does not have command-line selection. Unbound modifier combinations are
ignored. Cooked readers such as login keep their separate byte-input behavior.

Shoot groups runs of ASCII letters, digits, underscore, and valid non-ASCII
scalars into words. ASCII punctuation, spaces, and tabs separate words. This
is scalar-safe, not Unicode word segmentation. Word movement skips separators.
From inside a word, Alt+Left skips that word and moves to the preceding word's
start; Alt+Right skips it and moves to the next word's end. At a word boundary,
the adjacent word in the requested direction is selected. With no word in that
direction, movement stops at the command boundary. Word deletion removes the
same span that the corresponding motion would traverse.

Commands and scripts
--------------------

Shoot parses one command or assignment per line. Spaces and tabs separate
arguments. Single and double quotes group text and are removed. Quoted and
unquoted pieces can form one argument; empty quoted arguments are allowed.
Outside quotes, backslash escapes the next character; within either quote
style backslashes are ordinary text. An unquoted // starts a comment.

$name expands a shell variable outside single quotes. An unset variable
expands to an empty string. Unquoted argument patterns support *, ?, [abc],
[a-z], and [!abc]. Patterns match one path component at a time; results are
sorted, hidden names require a component beginning with ., and unmatched
patterns remain literal. Quoting or escaping wildcard syntax keeps it literal.
** does not recurse, and Shoot does not perform field splitting or command
substitution. There is no environment-variable vector or general tilde
expansion. ~ is expanded only in supported path-aware features such as jump,
script paths, redirection, and the exists condition.

Assignments use name = value. Names begin with an ASCII letter or underscore;
later characters may also be digits. A bounded integer update is supported:

    count = $count + 1
    count = $count - 1

Conditions use if, optional else, while, and end:

    if exists PATH
    if LEFT == RIGHT
    if INTEGER < INTEGER
    if INTEGER <= INTEGER
    if INTEGER > INTEGER
    if INTEGER >= INTEGER

String equality compares expanded text. Relational comparisons use signed
decimal integers. Compound conditions, break, and continue are not
available. Interactive blocks are collected through their matching end.
Scripts are checked for block structure before execution; a later command
failure can occur after earlier commands have already run.

Built-ins are jump, exit, help, history, and reload. Other commands
resolve from /bin or by an absolute executable path. Shoot reports launch,
wait, and crash failures. An ordinary nonzero child status remains a command
failure and stops a script without adding a duplicate generic diagnostic.
Arguments are passed separately to the child, not as an inherited environment.
Expanded vectors grow dynamically under the native 8 MiB budget, including
pointer slots and string terminators. Hundreds or thousands of matches can
be passed to one child when memory permits. Preparation errors occur before
execution or output-redirection side effects. Multi-file deletion itself is
not transactional: later filesystem failures can leave earlier files deleted.
Use ./* to keep matching names beginning with - from becoming command options.

One output redirection may be used on a command:

    command arguments > PATH
    command arguments >> PATH

> creates or truncates a regular file; >> creates a missing file or
appends to an existing one. Only standard output is redirected. There is no
input redirection, separate standard error, pipe, background job, command
chaining, or subshell.

For file commands, use copy SOURCE... DESTINATION and
move SOURCE... DESTINATION. With multiple sources, the destination must be an
existing directory. copy accepts regular files only. move uses filesystem
move safeguards and supports files and directories. plant NAME... creates
empty files; mkdir NAME... creates directories. del TARGET... removes files
and empty directories. Tree removal requires the explicit -r or --recursive
option. These batch commands report per-target failures and return failure if
any requested operation fails; a batch can partially succeed.

History and persistent state
----------------------------

The optional Shoot configuration is:

    ~/.config/shoot/config

Shoot does not create the file or its parent directories. To make a file,
first use jump to go to your home directory, create each missing directory
with mkdir, then open the new file in Reed, enter settings, and save with
/s. For example, if .config does not yet exist:

    jump
    mkdir .config
    mkdir .config/shoot
    reed .config/shoot/config

Omit a mkdir command for a directory that already exists. The configuration
is read at startup and can be reloaded with the reload built-in. Defaults
apply when the file is missing. Lines beginning with // are comments; blank
lines and whitespace around assignments are accepted. Boolean values are
true or false; colors must be one of the names below. Unknown keys are
ignored. A malformed line or invalid value for a recognized setting makes
reload fail and leaves the prior settings active.

    prompt_color = green
    completion_color = dark_gray
    completion_case_sensitive = true
    history_enabled = true
    history_limit = 256
    history_persist = true
    history_ignore_duplicates = true
    history_ignore_space = true

prompt_color and completion_color accept black, red, green,
yellow, blue, magenta, cyan, light_gray, dark_gray, bright_red,
bright_green, bright_yellow, bright_blue, bright_magenta,
bright_cyan, or white. The four history_* settings other than
history_limit, and completion_case_sensitive, are booleans.
history_limit is an integer from 1 through 4096 and controls the number of
in-memory entries retained.

Persistent command history is stored at:

    ~/.state/shoot/history

History is read when persistence is enabled and written on normal shell exit
or orderly system shutdown/reboot. Its parent directory is created only when
a write is needed. The history built-in displays current entries; options
include -c (clear memory), -d N (delete an entry), -a (append pending
entries), -n (import reliably new entries), -r (import the saved file), and
-w (replace saved contents with current memory). -w is destructive to the
saved history. Read history -h for quick usage and understand that filesystem
mutation failures may leave an uncertain result.

On upgrade, an old ~/.shoot/config is read if the new configuration is absent
and copied exclusively to the new path; the old file remains. If both exist,
the new file takes precedence. An old ~/.shoot/history is imported without
modifying either path. If it is the only saved history, the first write moves
it to the new path before appending. If both history files exist, old entries
are loaded before new entries and writes go only to the new file. Migration
never overwrites the destination or silently removes the old contents.

Limits and boundaries
---------------------

Interactive commands retain the 511 input-byte and 512 parsed word-byte limits.
Expanded arguments use the dynamic native process budget instead of a fixed
small argv array. Pathnames remain bounded to 255 UTF-8 bytes. Scripts are
limited to 4096 source lines, 512
statements, 511 bytes per line, and 64 nested blocks. Shoot's parser is a small
native shell language, not Bash or POSIX sh. History persistence is subject to
the filesystem's runtime mutation and durability guarantees; it is not a
power-failure transaction.
